<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#amarna-period">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amarna Period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1353–1336 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> Period is the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> in the mid-fourteenth century BCE, named after Amarna (Akhetaten), the new capital built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> for the cult of the sun disc <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>. Its archive of clay tablets, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, preserves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> rulers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, including the kings of Ugarit. Egypt returned to its traditional religion under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Amarna Period was the later part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, when the royal residence moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> to Akhetaten, meaning Horizon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, at the site of modern Amarna. The move took place under Amenhotep IV, who changed his name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> to reflect a religious change in which the sun disc Aten was worshipped above all other gods. Four kings reigned in the period: Akhenaten, Smenkhkare, Neferneferuaten and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a>. A leading theory holds that Smenkhkare was co-regent towards the end of Akhenaten's reign. Neferneferuaten was a female ruler, perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a> or the wife of Smenkhkare, and the reigns of both are not confirmed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>'s cult is the earliest verified expression of a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a>, though scholars debate its nature: some say he restored monotheism, others that he suppressed one solar cult by asserting another and never abandoned other traditional deities. Its best-known text is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a>. Royal portraiture changed too, showing Akhenaten in an androgynous, highly stylised manner, and builders used smaller blocks of stone set in strong mortar. Official inscriptions began to reflect the spoken language of the time. The royal women, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a>, who bore six daughters, left more surviving text than any other women of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna Letters</a> are the correspondence among the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a>, which modern historians have called the Club of Great Powers. Their letters follow a consistent formula, opening with a greeting and good wishes, and the rulers addressed each other as brothers. The gifts they exchanged, especially gold, are a constant subject, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> married a Mitanni princess to strengthen ties between the two states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> came to the throne at about nine years old, and the traditional pantheon was swiftly restored. Much of the government moved to Memphis. He died before he was twenty without a surviving heir, and Ay succeeded him. Ay's three-year reign was followed by that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a>, a general, who ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> torn down and cut the period from official histories and monuments, so that his monuments present him as the direct successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called the period the time of the enemy of Akhetaten, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> suffered damnatio memoriae. Horemheb appointed Paramessu, who became Ramesses I in 1292 BCE, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Nineteenth Dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amarna Period” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Amarna age</b> Ammishtamru I and Niqmaddu II corresponded with the pharaohs of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom, but around 1350 BCE Niqmaddu II accepted the overlordship of the Hittite Empire, sealing a treaty with Suppiluliuma I that fixed an annual tribute in gold, silver and fine…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Parallels in other royal tombs imply a somewhat standard set of object types, and no papyri were found, to the disappointment of Egyptologists who hoped for documents on the <b>Amarna Period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/tomb-of-tutankhamun">Tomb of Tutankhamun · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
